README.OSK -- GNU C 1.37.1 for OS-9/68000, as it is kept on this diskWhat this is  The source of the CERN distribution of GNU C 1.37.1 for OS-9/68000,  prepared by Julian Blake in February 1991 from Atsushi Seyama's port,  with changes by Martin Husemann and Kei Thomsen.  The binaries built  from it are in CMDS/GCC137.  README.osk in this directory is the  distribution's own account of how to build it; Build.osk,  Build-osk-native and Build-osk-cross (here Build_osk_native and  Build_osk_cross) give the steps.Where it came from  gcc-1.37.1-osk-src.tar.Z, gcc-1.37.1-osk-bin.tar.Z,  gcc-1.37.1-osk-doc.tar.Z and gcc-1.37.1-osk-README, as distributed  by anonymous FTP in 1991.Terms  The GNU General Public License, version 1 -- COPYING, in this  directory and in DOC/gcc137.  The distribution's README says: "The  GNU C compiler is free software.  Permission to copy is given in the  file COPYING which is included in each distribution file."What was changed, all of it  1. Line ends.  The archive was packed on a UNIX host with LF line     ends; every file here is CR-terminated, as OS-9 text is.  Nothing     else in any file was altered, so there is no ORIG directory: no     source file was edited.  2. File names.  36 files had a hyphen in their name.  They are     renamed with an underscore, which is exactly what the     distribution's own unix2osk script does and what README.osk says     to do under OS-9; the sources already #include the underscore     names when OSK is defined.  The 36:       Build-osk-cross Build-osk-native Makefile-osk-cross       Makefile-osk-native basic-block.h c-convert.c c-decl.c c-parse.h       c-parse.tab.c c-parse.y c-tree.h c-typeck.c caller-save.c       emit-rtl.c fold-const.c global-alloc.c hard-params.c       hard-reg-set.h init-cc68 local-alloc.c math-68881.h       move-if-change print-self.c print-self1.c print-tree.c       stor-layout.c va-i860.h va-mips.h va-osk.h va-pyr.h va-spur.h       config/out-osk.c config/tm-m68k.h config/tm-osk.h       config/xm-osk.h config/xm-vax.h  3. Left out of the source: c-parse.output and c-parse.tab.output,     339,704 bytes between them.  They are bison's verbose listings of     the parser tables -- produced by a build, read by nobody.     c-parse.tab.c, the parser itself, is kept, so the compiler builds     without bison as README.osk describes.  4. Left out of the binary distribution: cc1.stb, cccp.stb and gcc.stb,     symbol tables for a debugger.  5. Left out of the documentation distribution: gcc.dvi, gcc.ps,     cpp.dvi and cpp.ps, which are typeset from gcc.texinfo and     cpp.texinfo (both kept, in DOC/gcc137), and texinfo.tex and     texindex.c, which are TeX's tools rather than gcc's.  6. Module names.  The three programs register new module names,     matching their new file names:         gcc   ->  gcc137         (CMDS/GCC137/gcc137)         cccp  ->  gcc137_cccp    (CMDS/GCC137/gcc137_cccp)         cc1   ->  gcc137_cc1     (CMDS/GCC137/gcc137_cc1)     OS-9 answers by module name once a module is resident, and GCC139's     driver and passes already use gcc, cccp and cc1.  The new name is     appended to the module and M$Name pointed at it; no instruction     moves and the old name stays where it was, unreferenced.  The code     is the 1991 build, byte for byte.How it runs here  The driver looks for its passes where -B says, and the prefix includes  the start of the file name; it wants Microware's r68 and l68 resident  or in the execution directory, and the reader's own cstart.r,  clibn.l, math.l, sys.l and header files:    gcc137 -B/dd/CMDS/GCC137/gcc137_ -I/h1/DEFS -L/dd/GCCLIB -L/h1/LIB           -o hello hello.c  Its headers are in /dd/GCCDEFS and its gnulib.l in /dd/GCCLIB, the two  directories compiled into it.  All three programs use Microware's cio.